      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "UNASSIGNED",
          "Label": "Introduction",
          "content": "Bacterial meningitis may relapse after adequate antibiotic treatment. In most cases, however, the pathophysiology cannot be identified."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "UNASSIGNED",
          "Label": "Presentation of case",
          "content": "We describe a preterm infant with recurrent episodes of meningitis due to infection with an identical Escherichia coli strain both at birth and at 10 days after cessation of a 3 week course of appropriate antibiotic treatment. At the time of recurrence, the patient presented with fulminant severe cardiac failure due to acute myocarditis, coupled with a concurrent echovirus 18 infection (confirmed by stool culture and serological analysis)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "UNASSIGNED",
          "Label": "Conclusion",
          "content": "Co-infection by echovirus may underlie recurrence of Escherichia coli meningitis in this case."
        }
      ]},
